<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kongu Nadu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kongu Nadu is the historical region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, around the modern cities of Coimbatore, Erode, Salem and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karur">Karur</a>. A fertile upland crossed by trade routes between the east and west coasts, it was fought over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and later Vijayanagara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. Finds of Roman coin hoards show its importance in ancient trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kongu-nadu.jpg" alt="Kongu Nadu" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C1MM original file: Chumwa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kongu_Nadu_district_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name combines two Tamil words, Kongu country. Kongu may come from the konganam flowers worn as garlands by the early Kongars, or from kangu, edge or boundary, since the area lay on the borders of the ancient Tamil kingdoms; it was also called Kongu Mandalam. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> run down its western side and meet the Eastern Ghats in the Nilgiri mountains, and most of the region lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, which slopes from west to east. The Kaveri, Bhavani, Amaravati and Noyyal rivers rise in the Western Ghats and flow east, and the Palghat Gap links the region to the west coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Kodumanal on the Noyyal show settlement from the 4th century BCE, and the Patiṟṟuppattu of the 1st century CE describes it as a thriving industrial and commercial centre. The region was the eastern entrance to the Palakkad Gap on the trade route called Rajakesari Peruvazhi, which ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> on the west coast to Arikamedu on the east. Early Tamil Brahmi writing has been found on coins, seals and rings from the Amaravati river bed near Karuvur, which was the Chera capital when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> ruled the region between the 1st and 4th centuries.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> made incursions in the 7th century, and Rashtrakutas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a> held parts of it. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> the whole region came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> influence in the late 10th century; it was directly ruled by the Imperial Cholas until 1064 and then autonomously by the Kongu Cholas, who bore the title Konattar.</p><p>Later came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and, in the 15th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. After its fall <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> governors divided the region into 24 palayams. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> gained it in the late 18th century, and after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and the Polygar Wars the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> annexed it to the Madras Presidency in the early 19th century; in the Second Polygar War of 1801 Dheeran Chinnamalai fought the Company here.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The region covers roughly 60,895 square kilometres of western and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, with a population of over 27.4 million at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>; its borders have never been officially defined. Coimbatore, Tiruppur and Salem are the largest urban areas, and most people speak Kongu Tamil, a dialect of Tamil. Mudumalai, established in 1940 as the first national park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, lies within it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kongu Nadu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Aditya I defeated the Pallava and Pandyan dynasties early in the 10th century, capturing Tondaimandalam and <b>Kongu Nadu</b>, and Aditya's son Parantaka I defeated the Rashtrakuta dynasty in the battle of Vallala and the Pandyas again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vishnuvardhana’s victory at Talakad in 1116 broke Chola power in southern Karnataka, and his campaigns reached the <b>Kongu</b> region and Nolambavadi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory covered the Malabar coast and the <b>Kongu</b> country inland; their capital Vanchi is identified with Karur in Tamil Nadu by most historians.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-cheras">Early Cheras</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors expanded into the Kaveri delta and the <b>Kongu</b> country, competing with the Pallava dynasty and the Chalukyas of Badami.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-pandyan-empire">First Pandyan Empire</a></li></ul></section>
